# The lifetimes module (spec §19.2, §5.5): what the std's natives hold and keep, which no code shows
# the analysis of lifetimes (§16). Both annotations stand only on the std's declarations, since a host
# keeps no language values (§17.3).

# On an extern struct: a value of it holds what a value of `T` holds, a tuple listing several types
# and `()` none. Without it, a value holds its type arguments.
struct Holds<T>

# On a function: each parameter, by name, comes to hold the values of the parameters listed for it.
# Without it, a parameter taken by `&` keeps every other one, and nothing else is kept. On one with a
# body, it stands for the body, which calls code the analysis can't follow (the prelude's resolvers,
# deciding through the closures they hold): callers read the function as a native.
struct Keeps { into: Map<String, Array<String>> }
